nd along\nhighway 160 to Wolf Creek Pass.\n- Highway 160, between Wolf Creek Pass and South Fork, continues to\nbe closed. Continue to heed all road closures.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-23T23:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Mineral, CO; Rio Grande, CO; Saguache, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9764a293bbe6bc96215b6620007af67db5c69cb8.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.9764a293bbe6bc96215b6620007af67db5c69cb8.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 23 at 7:12PM MDT until October 24 at 10:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"...The Flood Warning is extended for the following rivers in\nColorado...\n\nRio Grande Near Del Norte affecting Rio Grande County.\n\n.High flows and flooding will continue along the Rio Grande through\nDel Norte.  The river levels will continue to fall but remain in\nminor flood stage into Saturday morning.\n\nFor the Upper Rio Grande River...including Del Norte...Minor flooding\nis forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Minor flooding is occurring and minor flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande  near Del Norte.\n\n* WHEN...Until Saturday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 6.5 feet, Moderate Flood. Water flows over low spots\nin Hanna Lane Road west of Del Norte. Water threatens homes and RV\npark near Del Norte Park in Del Norte. Homes near First Street and\nthe Oak Street Bridge on Colorado Highway 112 are threatened by\nhigh water.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 6:46 PM MDT Friday the stage was 6.4 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 5.0 feet.\n- Recent Activity...The maximum river stage in the 24 hours ending\nat 6:46 PM MDT Friday was 6.6 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to fall below flood stage late\ntomorrow morning and continue falling to 3.2 feet Wednesday\nevening.\n- Flood stage is 5.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 6.4\nfeet on 06/29/1927.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-24T22:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2278d6564e523f4ffadf9b13c28ddb6dc7687afe.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2278d6564e523f4ffadf9b13c28ddb6dc7687afe.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 23 at 5:44PM MDT until October 24 at 12:00AM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible although the risk will decrease into the evening as\nstronger showers and thunderstorms diminish.\n\n* WHERE...The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...Until midnight MDT tonight.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may exacerbate flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Waves of showers and isolated embedded thunderstorms will\ncontinue into the evening.  Although rainfall will be more\nlimited in coverage, localized amounts of up to 0.5 inches could\ncause additional minor rises in the smaller creeks and\ntributaries.\n- The eastern San Juan Mountains and Upper Rio Grande Valley with\nthe greatest risk across Conejos County.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-24T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.679cfcddd2bb5871197dfd101de29be63bb4d4a7.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.679cfcddd2bb5871197dfd101de29be63bb4d4a7.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-10-24T00:04:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KPUB 240004 AFDPUB Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Pueblo CO 604 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Heavy rain threat will develop over the eastern plains on Saturday into Sunday. - Some accumulation snow possible across the higher peaks Saturday afternoon and evening. - Mainly dry th